 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of how lookups are served. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookups are served from a **local embedded SQLite index** that lives on the kiosk. The index is a derived artifact: it is built on the kiosk from the records in a validated snapshot and is never transferred between kiosks. The signed snapshot remains the authoritative input and the only update channel; the index carries no authority of its own. Nothing outside this node sees the database file, its schema, or the query path.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Import proceeds in a fixed order so a failure at any step leaves the active snapshot untouched:
+
+1. Verify the signature using a bundled public key.
+2. Reject unsupported schema versions.
+3. Validate record serial numbers for uniqueness.
+4. Build a fresh SQLite index from the snapshot records into a temporary database file, separate from the active one.
+5. Verify the built index against the snapshot (for example, that the indexed record count matches the snapshot's record count) before it can serve lookups.
+6. Activate the new snapshot and its index together by an atomic swap, so lookups see either the whole previous snapshot or the whole new one and never a partially imported set.
+
+If any step fails — including the index build or its verification — the import is rejected, the previous snapshot and its index stay active, and the system reports a useful error. A temporary index from a failed import is discarded and never activated.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize the query serial by trimming surrounding whitespace and uppercasing ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation and internal characters remain significant. Resolve the normalized serial through the SQLite index as an exact-match, single-record query; the index makes this lookup fast enough to meet the kiosk resolution-time goal over the full record set. Return the active snapshot date on every result, whether a record is found or not.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built per-kiosk from each snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory serving one central index; managed search service | The kiosks have no network route during normal operation, so only an on-kiosk backend serves lookups without a runtime network dependency. SQLite gives predictable exact-match lookups with no service to run; the cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on import, which stays inside this node. The two networked alternatives were reconsidered for lower per-kiosk index maintenance and room for richer search, but both would add a runtime network dependency and, for the managed service, recurring cost and credentials — all excluded by the offline tenets. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether uniqueness validation (step 3) applies to raw serials or to the normalized lookup key. Raw serials that are distinct but normalize to the same key (e.g. `abc` and `ABC`) pass raw-serial uniqueness yet collide in the index. See the Phase 2 edge-probe item raised with the user before specs are revised.
+2. Whether a corrupted active index discovered at kiosk startup (e.g. after power loss during a prior swap) should trigger a rebuild from the retained snapshot, or block lookups until the next import.
